# =============================================================================
# wrld.design — response headers
#
# Consumed by Cloudflare Workers static assets. This file is CONFIGURATION,
# not content: it is never served at a public URL (verified — GET /_headers
# returns 404). Copied verbatim into dist/ by scripts/build_site.mjs.
#
# Limits: 100 rules, 2,000 characters per line.
#
# THE RULE THAT BITES — read this before editing.
# There is no "most specific wins". EVERY matching rule applies, and when two
# matching rules set the SAME header name, the values are APPENDED with a
# comma rather than replaced. An earlier revision of this file set
# Cache-Control in both `/tokens/*` and `/*.ts`, and because
# tokens/tokens.ts matches both it was served with:
#
#   Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400, public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400
#
# — a malformed header on a URL that other WRLD sites treat as a contract.
#
# So the invariant below is deliberate: every Cache-Control rule matches a
# DISJOINT set of paths, and where rules do overlap on path they set disjoint
# header NAMES (the Content-Type block sets no Cache-Control, and vice
# versa). If you ever genuinely need two rules to set the same header, unset
# it first in the narrower rule with `! Header-Name` — bang plus a MANDATORY
# space; `!Header` has no colon and silently discards the whole rule.
#
# Also: only one `*` per rule path, a value line must never start with `/`
# (the parser reads that as a new rule path), query strings cannot be
# matched, and redirects are evaluated before headers.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Baseline, every path.
#
# CORS is open by design. wrld.design is an asset host: other WRLD origins
# load tokens.css, the self-hosted typefaces, and logo SVGs from here, and
# @font-face fetches are ALWAYS CORS-mode — a .ttf served without
# Access-Control-Allow-Origin will not render even between our own
# properties. An assets-only Worker cannot vary the value per request:
# _headers supports only `*`, `null`, or one exact literal origin, with no
# wildcard patterns. Everything served here is public, read-only,
# unauthenticated reference material, and `*` additionally forbids
# credentialed requests — so it is the correct answer, not a shortcut.
# Narrowing to an allowlist later means giving this Worker a script
# entrypoint, and billable requests.
#
# Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy is required by consumers whose pages are
# cross-origin isolated (COEP: require-corp); without it those pages cannot
# load these fonts or logos at all.
#
# Access-Control-Allow-Methods and Access-Control-Max-Age are deliberately
# ABSENT. Verified: the asset layer answers OPTIONS with 405 Method Not
# Allowed even when they are declared, so a CORS preflight can never succeed
# here and advertising methods would be fiction. Every real consumer request
# — a stylesheet, a font, an <img>, a fetch() without custom headers — is a
# simple request that never preflights.
#
# Deliberately NOT set:
#   Content-Security-Policy — the styleguide and UI kits load React and
#     Babel-standalone from unpkg.com and evaluate inline JSX, so any
#     workable policy needs both 'unsafe-inline' and 'unsafe-eval' and blocks
#     no realistic attack. Self-hosting those is the prerequisite for a CSP
#     worth having.
#   X-Frame-Options / frame-ancestors — the previews are designed to be
#     embedded in Craft docs and design tools. Nothing here is authenticated
#     and nothing changes state, so framing carries no clickjacking risk
#     worth breaking that for.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin
  Timing-Allow-Origin: *
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Typefaces — immutable for a year, listed by EXACT filename.
#
# Enumerated rather than globbed as /fonts/*, deliberately. Header rules are
# applied to 404 responses too, so a /fonts/* immutable rule poisons browser
# caches with year-long, never-revalidated 404s: verified that
# GET /fonts/DoesNotExist-Bold.ttf under a glob returned
#   404 + Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
# meaning a client that ever requests a wrong font path would cache that
# failure for a year, and adding the file later would not fix it for them.
# Add a line here only when the file actually exists.
#
# `immutable` tells browsers never to revalidate. That is a versioning
# commitment: replacing a typeface means shipping a NEW filename, never
# overwriting one in place.
#
# No Content-Type override — verified .ttf is served natively as font/ttf,
# so an override would be redundant, and under a glob it also mislabelled
# the 404 HTML body as a font while nosniff was set.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/fonts/Montserrat-VariableFont_wght.ttf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/Ubuntu-Light.ttf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/Ubuntu-Regular.ttf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/Ubuntu-Medium.ttf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/Ubuntu-Bold.ttf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/UbuntuMono-Regular.ttf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/UbuntuMono-Bold.ttf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Brand artwork — a day fresh, then served stale while revalidating.
#
# Shorter than the fonts because marks genuinely do get corrected in place,
# and a consumer hotlinking one should pick that up without a rename.
# stale-while-revalidate is honoured by Chromium and by Cloudflare's cache;
# other browsers simply revalidate, which is cheap thanks to the ETag.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/logos/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tokens and stylesheets — the contract other sites depend on.
#
# These are the files production WRLD pages link directly, and they are not
# fingerprinted, so correct propagation matters more than saving a request.
# An hour of freshness with a day of stale-while-revalidate means a token fix
# reaches every consuming site within the hour without any visitor blocking
# on a revalidation round trip. Do not lengthen this to match /fonts/. If a
# consumer ever needs year-long immutable caching, publish a versioned path
# (/v1/tokens/tokens.css) and give that path its own rule.
#
# /tokens/* sets ONLY Cache-Control. tokens/tokens.ts also matches /*.ts
# below, which sets only Content-Type — disjoint header names, so nothing
# appends. Keep it that way.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/styles.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400

/colors_and_type.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400

/tokens/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400

/_ds_bundle.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400

/_ds_manifest.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Content-Type corrections. No rule here sets Cache-Control, so none of them
# can collide with the blocks above.
#
# Left alone, Cloudflare derives Content-Type from the file extension and
# gets these wrong for source that is published to be READ:
#   .ts    -> video/mp2t. Verified. That is the correct registration for the
#            extension (MPEG transport stream) and useless here. It hits
#            tokens/tokens.ts — a published entry point in package.json
#            "exports" — and every .d.ts under ui_kits/. A browser would
#            offer them as video downloads.
#   .jsx   -> text/jsx. Verified. Browsers download rather than display.
#   .md    -> text/markdown. Same.
#   LICENSE and any extensionless file -> no Content-Type at all. Verified.
#
# There is no registered MIME type for TypeScript; text/plain is the honest
# choice and makes these display in a browser. Note the one-way door: with
# nosniff set, a text/plain file can never be loaded as an executable module
# script. If a .jsx or .ts here ever needs to be a real <script type="module">
# it needs a JavaScript MIME type instead. Nothing does today — the
# styleguide's .jsx is fetched by Babel, not by the browser's script loader.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/*.ts
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

/*.jsx
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

/*.md
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

/LICENSE
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Neutralise script inside SVG on direct navigation. An SVG rendered through
# <img> or CSS cannot execute script, but one opened at its own URL is a
# document and can. These are our own marks, so this is belt and braces — and
# it costs nothing.
#
# If a site-wide Content-Security-Policy is ever added to the /* block above,
# add `! Content-Security-Policy` as the first line of this rule, or SVGs
# will receive both policies comma-joined.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/*.svg
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; sandbox

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Note on HTML and Markdown caching: no explicit Cache-Control rule. Verified
# that Cloudflare's default for assets is already
# "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate" plus an ETag, which is exactly what
# these documents want. An earlier revision stated it explicitly, but the
# rule was inert twice over — the value was byte-identical to the default,
# and a `/*.html` pattern does not match the site's real entry points anyway,
# since `/` and `/styleguide/` are directory URLs with no `.html` in the path.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keep build preview hostnames out of search results.
#
# wrld.design is the only canonical origin, but preview_urls is enabled in
# wrangler.jsonc, so <version>-wrld-design-system.<subdomain>.workers.dev is
# publicly reachable and would otherwise be indexable — duplicate content
# against the canonical site.
#
# Verified locally that this rule parses AND that it does not leak onto
# non-workers.dev hosts: X-Robots-Tag is absent from /, /styleguide/, and
# /tokens/tokens.css, including when the request carries a
# `Host: wrld.design` header. That containment is the important half — a
# stray noindex on the apex would deindex the real site.
#
# The half that CANNOT be verified without a real preview deploy is whether
# it actually fires on a live *.workers.dev preview URL. Check that on the
# first non-production build (see docs/CLOUDFLARE_DEPLOY.md §4). If it does
# not fire, the fallback is setting preview_urls to false and giving up
# per-branch review URLs.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
https://:version.:subdomain.workers.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
